Q: Calculating days between two dates with Java A: <h2>UPDATE</h2>
<p><em>The original answer from 2013 is now outdated because some of the classes have been replaced. The new way of doing this is using the new <a href="https://www.oracle.com/technical-resources/articles/java/jf14-date-time.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>java.time</code></a> classes.</em></p>
<h3>24-hour days</h3>
<pre><code>DateTimeFormatter dtf = DateTimeFormatter.ofPattern(&quot;dd MM yyyy&quot;);
String inputString1 = &quot;23 01 1997&quot;;
String inputString2 = &quot;27 04 1997&quot;;

try {
    LocalDateTime date1 = LocalDate.parse(inputString1, dtf).atStartOfDay();
    LocalDateTime date2 = LocalDate.parse(inputString2, dtf).atStartOfDay();
    long daysBetween = Duration.between(date1, date2).toDays();
    System.out.println (&quot;Days: &quot; + daysBetween);
} catch (DateTimeParseException e) {
    e.printStackTrace();
}
</code></pre>
<h3>Calendar days</h3>
<p>Note that the solution above counts days as generic chunks of 24 hours, not calendar days.</p>
<p>For calendar days, use <a href="https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/23/docs/api/java.base/java/time/temporal/ChronoUnit.html#DAYS" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>java.time.temporal.ChronoUnit.DAYS</code></a> and its <a href="https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/23/docs/api/java.base/java/time/temporal/ChronoUnit.html#between(java.time.temporal.Temporal,java.time.temporal.Temporal)" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>between</code></a> method.</p>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>long daysBetween = ChronoUnit.DAYS.between(date1, date2) ;
</code></pre>
<hr />
<h2>Original answer (outdated as of Java 8)</h2>
<p>You are making some conversions with your Strings that are not necessary. There is a <a href="https://docs.oracle.com/javase/8/docs/api/java/text/SimpleDateFormat.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>SimpleDateFormat</code></a> class for it - try this:</p>
<pre><code>SimpleDateFormat myFormat = new SimpleDateFormat(&quot;dd MM yyyy&quot;);
String inputString1 = &quot;23 01 1997&quot;;
String inputString2 = &quot;27 04 1997&quot;;

try {
    Date date1 = myFormat.parse(inputString1);
    Date date2 = myFormat.parse(inputString2);
    long diff = date2.getTime() - date1.getTime();
    System.out.println (&quot;Days: &quot; + TimeUnit.DAYS.convert(diff, TimeUnit.MILLISECONDS));
} catch (ParseException e) {
    e.printStackTrace();
}
</code></pre>
<p>There have been some discussions regarding the correctness of this code. It does indeed take care of leap years. However, the <a href="https://docs.oracle.com/javase/7/docs/api/java/util/concurrent/TimeUnit.html#convert(long,%20java.util.concurrent.TimeUnit)" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>TimeUnit.DAYS.convert</code></a> function loses precision since milliseconds are converted to days (see the linked doc for more info). If this is a problem, <code>diff</code> can also be converted by hand:</p>
<pre><code>float days = (diff / (1000*60*60*24));
</code></pre>
<p>Note that this is a <code>float</code> value, not necessarily an <code>int</code>.</p>

Q: Problems trying to prevent sleeping of display on my web app A: <p>You can use the <strong>Screen Wake Lock API</strong> to prevent the display from going to sleep while the page is active.</p>
<p>Below is a React hook example that:</p>
<ul>
<li><p>requests a screen wake lock</p>
</li>
<li><p>re-requests it when the tab becomes visible again</p>
</li>
<li><p>safely handles race conditions using an <code>isMountedRef</code></p>
</li>
</ul>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>import { useEffect, useRef, useCallback, RefObject } from 'react';

export const useScreenWakeLock = () =&gt; {
  const wakeLockRef = useRef&lt;WakeLockSentinel | null&gt;(null);

  const requestWakeLock = useCallback(
    async (isMountedRef: RefObject&lt;boolean&gt;) =&gt; {
      if (!('wakeLock' in navigator)) return;

      try {
        if (wakeLockRef.current &amp;&amp; !wakeLockRef.current.released) return;

        const sentinel = await navigator.wakeLock.request('screen');

        if (!isMountedRef.current) {
          await sentinel.release();
          return;
        }

        wakeLockRef.current = sentinel;
      } catch (err) {
        console.error('Wake Lock request failed:', err);
      }
    },
    [],
  );

  useEffect(() =&gt; {
    const isMountedRef = { current: true };

    requestWakeLock(isMountedRef);

    const handleVisibilityChange = () =&gt; {
      if (document.visibilityState === 'visible' &amp;&amp; isMountedRef.current) {
        requestWakeLock(isMountedRef);
      }
    };

    document.addEventListener('visibilitychange', handleVisibilityChange);

    return () =&gt; {
      isMountedRef.current = false;
      document.removeEventListener('visibilitychange', handleVisibilityChange);

      if (wakeLockRef.current) {
        wakeLockRef.current.release();
        wakeLockRef.current = null;
      }
    };
  }, [requestWakeLock]);
};
</code></pre>
<p><strong>References:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><p>MDN: <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/WakeLock" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/WakeLock</a></p>
</li>
<li><p>Browser support: <a href="https://caniuse.com/?search=WakeLock" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://caniuse.com/?search=WakeLock</a></p>
</li>
</ul>

Q: In .NET MAUI, ScrollView with nested CollectionView adds unexpected bottom whitespace on iOS – why? A: <p>You are nesting scrolling controls. From maui docs:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>ScrollView objects should not be nested. In addition, ScrollView objects should not be nested with other controls that provide scrolling, such as CollectionView, ListView, and WebView.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>If you want to show a header in your <code>CollectionView</code>, you should be using <code>CollectionView.Header</code>:</p>
<pre class="lang-xml prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;CollectionView ItemsSource=&quot;{Binding Items}&quot; BackgroundColor=&quot;LightGray&quot;&gt;
  &lt;CollectionView.Header&gt;
    &lt;Label Text=&quot;Header&quot; FontSize=&quot;24&quot; Margin=&quot;0,0,0,16&quot; /&gt;
  &lt;/CollectionView.Header&gt;
  &lt;CollectionView.ItemTemplate&gt;
    &lt;DataTemplate&gt;
      &lt;Frame Margin=&quot;0,0,0,8&quot; Padding=&quot;12&quot;&gt;
        &lt;Label Text=&quot;{Binding .}&quot; /&gt;
      &lt;/Frame&gt;
    &lt;/DataTemplate&gt;
  &lt;/CollectionView.ItemTemplate&gt;
&lt;/CollectionView&gt;
</code></pre>
<hr />
<p>Also keep in mind you're using <code>Frame</code> which is depercated, you should be using <code>Border</code> instead.</p>

Q: How accurate is the == function with floats with different decimals A: <p>It looks like you're trying to &quot;solve&quot; an equation by basically just trying a bunch of different values for <code>k6</code> and <code>k7</code>.  That's not necessarily a good way of doing it, but it can work, and it's actually a lot simpler than you made it look.</p>
<p>First, let's clear up a couple of misconceptions.  You wrote:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>How accurate is the <code>==</code> function</p>
</blockquote>
<p>It is very accurate.  (Actually it's an &quot;operator&quot;, not a function.)</p>
<blockquote>
<p>with floats with different decimals</p>
</blockquote>
<p>But this doesn't mean anything.  Floats don't <em>have</em> decimals.</p>
<p>The <code>==</code> operator tells you whether two vales are <em>exactly equal</em>.  But since you're &quot;shooting in the dark&quot; for values which might be &quot;close enough&quot; but will usually <em>not</em> be exactly equal, the <code>==</code> operator is definitely not what you want here.  (See also <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/questions/10334688">this question</a>.)</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The challenge I had was to round the values of the equation, and work with counters with decimal values.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Is that a challenge someone gave you, or a challenge you set yourself?  Working with counters with &quot;decimal values&quot; is probably an okay thing to try.  But rounding the values -- that doesn't make much sense, and there are better ways.</p>
<p>So with that said, here's a drastically simplified version of your program:</p>
<pre><code>int main()
{
    float C1 = -2783.02, C2 = -3921.87, j = 2.33, W = 801.15, xs = 1.04, k6, k7, equation;

    for (k6 = -2000; k6 &lt;= -1900; k6 += 0.1)
    {
        for (k7 = -1000; k7 &lt;= 1000; k7 += 0.1)
            {
                equation = (C1/(j*j))*(sin(xs/j))/(xs) + (C2/(j*j))*(cos(xs/j))/(xs) - (W*j*j/2)/(xs) - k7/(xs);

                if(close_enough(k6, equation))
                {
                    printf(&quot;k6 = %f and k7 = %f (eq = %f)\n&quot;, k6, k7, equation);
                }
            }
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>This runs <code>k6</code> from -2000 to -1900 by 0.1, and <code>k7</code> from -1000 to 1000, also by 0.1.  It computes the <code>equation</code> value just like your program did, but then it just looks to see if the equation value it got is or isn't &quot;close enough&quot; to the <code>k6</code> value.  (More on this in a minute.). When I run this, I get lots of output, beginning with:</p>
<pre><code>k6 = -2000.000000 and k7 = -967.607910 (eq = -1999.995239)
k6 = -1999.900024 and k7 = -967.707886 (eq = -1999.899170)
k6 = -1999.800049 and k7 = -967.807861 (eq = -1999.802979)
k6 = -1999.700073 and k7 = -967.907837 (eq = -1999.706909)
k6 = -1999.600098 and k7 = -968.007812 (eq = -1999.610718)
k6 = -1999.500122 and k7 = -968.107788 (eq = -1999.514648)
k6 = -1999.400146 and k7 = -968.207764 (eq = -1999.418457)
k6 = -1999.300171 and k7 = -968.307739 (eq = -1999.322388)
k6 = -1999.200195 and k7 = -968.407715 (eq = -1999.226196)
k6 = -1999.100220 and k7 = -968.507690 (eq = -1999.130127)
</code></pre>
<p>This shows the, for example, when we evaluate the equation with <code>k7 = -967.6</code>, we get an equation value of <code>-1999.995239</code>, which is pretty close to one of the trial <code>k6</code> values of <code>-2000</code>.  But this isn't the only pair of values that works: as other comments and answers have asserted, and as this program shows, there are lots (basically an infinite number) of pairs of values that work.</p>
<p>A few last thoughts:</p>
<ul>
<li>Floating point numbers are <em>not</em> decimal fractions internally.  That's how we print them out, but it's best to think of them as being able to represent reasonably arbitrary <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Real_number" rel="nofollow noreferrer">real numbers</a>.  (In fact, they're represented internally in binary, not decimal, which explains a lot of their seeming anomalies.)</li>
<li>Floating point numbers don't have infinite precision.  That's why , for example, the <code>k6</code> and <code>k7</code> values printed out by my program above didn't end in exactly <code>.1</code>, <code>.2</code>, <code>.3</code>, ... .  (Using type <code>double</code> instead of <code>float</code> would help.)</li>
<li>You might be wondering why I chose to run <code>k6</code> from -2000 to -1900.  The answer: I cheated; I knew that's where it would find answers.</li>
<li>Finally, here's the magic <code>close_enough</code> function called by my program above:</li>
</ul>
<p> </p>
<pre><code>int close_enough(float a, float b)
{
    if(fabs(a - b) &lt; 0.05)
         return 1;
    else return 0;
}
</code></pre>
<p>This function simply subtracts the two numbers being compared, takes the absolute value (<code>fabs</code>) to make the difference always positive, then asks whether the difference is less than 0.05.  (Since we're running through our <code>k6</code> and <code>k7</code> values by increments of 0.1, we can expect to find answers matching within half of that.) Please note that this is <em>not</em> a good way in general of checking for &quot;close enough&quot; equality, which is actually a surprisingly deep question.  This is an exceptionally simpleminded &quot;close enough equality&quot; function, suitable only for this rather contrived example.  (See more on this topic at <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/questions/17333">this question</a> and <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/questions/13940316">this question</a>.)</p>
<p>There's much more that could be said about all four of these last points, but this should get you started.</p>
